{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RCgs5msyk2Mz8kDP1bFQeW","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: rel01.3-uc001-du\n     2→title: \"Report recursive directory disk usage via du\"\n     3→\n     4→summary: |\n     5→  The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory to report disk usage. The\n     6→  differential testing harness runs both the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary\n     7→  (gdu from coreutils) on the same directory on the same filesystem with the same arguments\n     8→  and environment, and confirms that their outputs are identical byte-for-byte. Block counts\n     9→  are deterministic when both binaries stat the same files on the same filesystem. All tests\n    10→  use -k to pin output to 1024-byte blocks and eliminate platform block-size default variance.\n    11→\n    12→actor: Human Operator\n    13→\n    14→trigger: |\n    15→  The operator has completed the PRD (prd009-du.yaml) and shared component PRDs\n    16→  (pkg/sys, pkg/format from rel00.0) and invokes do-work to produce a passing differential\n    17→  test for cmd/du.\n    18→\n    19→flow:\n    20→  - id: F1\n    21→    step: |\n    22→      The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory with known file sizes and\n    23→      directory structure. Example: ./bin/du -k testdata/du-fixture/\n    24→  - id: F2\n    25→    step: |\n    26→      cmd/du traverses the directory tree using os.ReadDir (or filepath.Walk), calling\n    27→      pkg/sys.Lstat on each entry to obtain st_blocks for block counts and st_dev/st_ino\n    28→      for hard-link deduplication. It accumulates block counts per directory.\n    29→  - id: F3\n    30→    step: |\n    31→      cmd/du writes one line per directory (or per file with -a) in the format\n    32→      \"SIZE\\tPATH\\n\". With -h, sizes are formatted via pkg/format.HumanSize.\n    33→  - id: F4\n    34→    step: |\n    35→      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/du and gdu with\n    36→      the same arguments, environment, and working directory.\n    37→  - id: F5\n    38→    step: |\n    39→      The harness compares stdout byte-for-byte with no normalization. Both binaries must\n    40→      produce identical size values and path listings because they stat the same files on\n    41→      the same filesystem.\n    42→  - id: F6\n    43→    step: |\n    44→      If the outputs match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n    45→      triggering args, both outputs, and both exit codes.\n    46→\n    47→touchpoints:\n    48→  - T1: \"cmd/du — recursive traversal, block accumulation, hard-link dedup, flag modes (prd009-du R1, R2, R3, R4, R5)\"\n    49→  - T2: \"pkg/sys — Stat/Lstat for st_blocks, st_dev, st_ino, InstallSIGPIPEHandler\"\n    50→  - T3: \"pkg/format — HumanSize for -h output\"\n    51→  - T4: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, binary execution and comparison\"\n    52→\n    53→success_criteria:\n    54→  - id: S1\n    55→    criterion: cmd/du compiles and ./bin/du exists after mage build.\n    56→  - id: S2\n    57→    criterion: |\n    58→      Default recursive mode produces one line per subdirectory and the root directory,\n    59→      matching gdu -k output on the same fixture.\n    60→  - id: S3\n    61→    criterion: |\n    62→      -s summary mode produces one line per argument with the total size, matching gdu -sk\n    63→      output.\n    64→  - id: S4\n    65→    criterion: |\n    66→      -a all-files mode produces one line per file and directory, matching gdu -ak output.\n    67→  - id: S5\n    68→    criterion: |\n    69→      -h human-readable mode produces size strings (K/M/G) that match gdu -h output on the\n    70→      same fixture.\n    71→  - id: S6\n    72→    criterion: |\n    73→      -c grand total mode produces a \"total\" line after all arguments, matching gdu -ck output.\n    74→  - id: S7\n    75→    criterion: |\n    76→      Hard-link deduplication works: a fixture with two directory entries pointing to the same\n    77→      inode produces a total that counts the file's blocks only once, matching gdu behavior.\n    78→  - id: S8\n    79→    criterion: |\n    80→      --apparent-size mode uses st_size instead of st_blocks, matching gdu --apparent-size\n    81→      output.\n    82→  - id: S9\n    83→    criterion: |\n    84→      The differential test passes for all flag combinations: default -k, -sk, -ak, -h,\n    85→      -ck, -d 1, --apparent-size, and missing path.\n    86→\n    87→out_of_scope:\n    88→  - Cross-filesystem behavior (files on different mount points) is not tested in the\n    89→    differential harness; both binaries see the same single filesystem.\n    90→  - SIGPIPE handling is specified in prd009-du R5.1 but is not directly testable in the\n    91→    differential harness.\n    92→  - Sparse file creation depends on the filesystem; --apparent-size tests use a regular file\n    93→    with known size rather than a true sparse file.\n    94→\n    95→test_suite: test-rel01.3\n    96→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d21f8c7e-2c67-4416-ab87-bc43899a6f6f","uuid":"f7256caf-247b-4b18-9c3d-f59c434024e7","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/132/docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.3-uc001-du.yaml","content":"id: rel01.3-uc001-du\ntitle: \"Report recursive directory disk usage via du\"\n\nsummary: |\n  The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory to report disk usage. The\n  differential testing harness runs both the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary\n  (gdu from coreutils) on the same directory on the same filesystem with the same arguments\n  and environment, and confirms that their outputs are identical byte-for-byte. Block counts\n  are deterministic when both binaries stat the same files on the same filesystem. All tests\n  use -k to pin output to 1024-byte blocks and eliminate platform block-size default variance.\n\nactor: Human Operator\n\ntrigger: |\n  The operator has completed the PRD (prd009-du.yaml) and shared component PRDs\n  (pkg/sys, pkg/format from rel00.0) and invokes do-work to produce a passing differential\n  test for cmd/du.\n\nflow:\n  - id: F1\n    step: |\n      The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory with known file sizes and\n      directory structure. Example: ./bin/du -k testdata/du-fixture/\n  - id: F2\n    step: |\n      cmd/du traverses the directory tree using os.ReadDir (or filepath.Walk), calling\n      pkg/sys.Lstat on each entry to obtain st_blocks for block counts and st_dev/st_ino\n      for hard-link deduplication. It accumulates block counts per directory.\n  - id: F3\n    step: |\n      cmd/du writes one line per directory (or per file with -a) in the format\n      \"SIZE\\tPATH\\n\". With -h, sizes are formatted via pkg/format.HumanSize.\n  - id: F4\n    step: |\n      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/du and gdu with\n      the same arguments, environment, and working directory.\n  - id: F5\n    step: |\n      The harness compares stdout byte-for-byte with no normalization. Both binaries must\n      produce identical size values and path listings because they stat the same files on\n      the same filesystem.\n  - id: F6\n    step: |\n      If the outputs match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n      triggering args, both outputs, and both exit codes.\n\ntouchpoints:\n  - T1: \"cmd/du — recursive traversal, block accumulation, hard-link dedup, flag modes (prd009-du R1, R2, R3, R4, R5)\"\n  - T2: \"pkg/sys — Stat/Lstat for st_blocks, st_dev, st_ino, InstallSIGPIPEHandler\"\n  - T3: \"pkg/format — HumanSize for -h output\"\n  - T4: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, binary execution and comparison\"\n\nsuccess_criteria:\n  - id: S1\n    criterion: cmd/du compiles and ./bin/du exists after mage build.\n  - id: S2\n    criterion: |\n      Default recursive mode produces one line per subdirectory and the root directory,\n      matching gdu -k output on the same fixture.\n  - id: S3\n    criterion: |\n      -s summary mode produces one line per argument with the total size, matching gdu -sk\n      output.\n  - id: S4\n    criterion: |\n      -a all-files mode produces one line per file and directory, matching gdu -ak output.\n  - id: S5\n    criterion: |\n      -h human-readable mode produces size strings (K/M/G) that match gdu -h output on the\n      same fixture.\n  - id: S6\n    criterion: |\n      -c grand total mode produces a \"total\" line after all arguments, matching gdu -ck output.\n  - id: S7\n    criterion: |\n      Hard-link deduplication works: a fixture with two directory entries pointing to the same\n      inode produces a total that counts the file's blocks only once, matching gdu behavior.\n  - id: S8\n    criterion: |\n      --apparent-size mode uses st_size instead of st_blocks, matching gdu --apparent-size\n      output.\n  - id: S9\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test passes for all flag combinations: default -k, -sk, -ak, -h,\n      -ck, -d 1, --apparent-size, and missing path.\n\nout_of_scope:\n  - Cross-filesystem behavior (files on different mount points) is not tested in the\n    differential harness; both binaries see the same single filesystem.\n  - SIGPIPE handling is specified in prd009-du R5.1 but is not directly testable in the\n    differential harness.\n  - Sparse file creation depends on the filesystem; --apparent-size tests use a regular file\n    with known size rather than a true sparse file.\n\ntest_suite: test-rel01.3\n","numLines":96,"startLine":1,"totalLines":96}}}
